+# Emotiscope — running SMC-Mixer control card
+
+THE TITAN MUST ALWAYS BE VISUALISING MUSIC. Normal default output is 100/255; explicit operator dimming/Pause remains available.
+
+Current connection: **SMC-Mixer Bluetooth**, not USB. Dashboard already opened at http://127.0.0.1:8776. The controller starts by reading the board; it does not send defaults or initial fader positions.
+
+| Control | Action |
+|---|---|
+| Faders 1–8 | Donor brightness; softness; speed; background; colour; colour range; saturation; warmth |
+| Knob 1 | Separate output brightness, 0–255 |
+| M1–M7 | Analog; Spectrum; Octave; Metronome; Spectronome; Hype; Bloom |
+| S1–S5 | Mirror; reverse colour range; automatic colour; screensaver; dithering |
+| Previous / Next | Previous/next musical mode, only 0–6 |
+| Play / Pause | Explicit output on/off; does not change the brightness setting |
+| SQ1–SQ8 | Recall compatible saved scene; empty slot does nothing |
+
+Faders and Knob1 use soft pickup: cross the existing value before taking control. For the present native saturation0.75, Fader7 picks up around56% travel. Move through that position, then move farther to change saturation. Knob1's normal default100/255 corresponds to roughly39% travel; subsequent operator input changed the live level. Read the dashboard for the current value. Buttons require a release before a fresh press; the first press after attachment may only establish that release cycle.
+
+**Fader1 and Fader4 are currently blocked** pending isolated labelled evidence for Fader1, Fader4, R5 and R8. Unlabelled movement cannot release that gate. The dashboard contains the capture workflow; its final persistence hardening remains open in the repair closeout, so full release qualification is not claimed.
+
+Speed is meaningful in Analog, not an invented global tempo control; this donor Bloom variant does not use it effectively. Reverse colour range has no visible effect at zero colour range. Donor brightness is not the hardware output gain and zero donor brightness is not blackout.
+
+The UI's scene-SQ1 save button records the confirmed state on the Mac, not MCU storage. Saving does not send a target patch. Physical scene and all-control qualification remain outstanding.
